aarch64 #23 milestone-4: fork child skips the ciderd connect + checkin (flag-on)

The post-fork child rebuilt its dserver RPC socket and checked in. Flag-on a self-contained child
needs no ciderd connection at all until it execs (mldr rebuilds the socket and checks in there) or
exits, so skip the whole block: socket refresh, lifetime-pipe refresh, guards, and checkin.

This is what 3 sessions of the checkin-abort chase missed. Skipping ONLY the checkin RPC while KEEPING
the socket refresh left a half-open dserver connection (a fresh socket ciderd sees, with no checkin
handshake) that made a fork+exec child (path_helper in the login chain) abort on teardown and
group-signal its siblings. Dropping the ENTIRE block removes the half-open state and the abort with it.

No leak: a self-contained child makes zero RPCs, so ciderd never sees it and allocates nothing to
reap; a child that execs gets mldr's checkin + the #33 death-watch. Verified: 200 pure-fork subshells
flag-on make 0 ciderd RPCs (RECV stays at the login-chain baseline) and finish faster than flag-off;
a 100-exec bash loop runs clean, no leak. shell /usr/bin/true checkin 10 -> 6 (RECV 16 -> 12), rc=0,
soak 3/3; flag-off unchanged (132). Flag-gated. On top of 0063.

